ket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   Ticket Number: SOL-84213\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution Notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is open, displaying the ticket resolution page.\n    *   The console has a dark blue background with white text and icons.\n    *   The top-left corner features the CoreID logo and the words \"Admin Console\" in white text.\n    *   The top-right corner displays the user's name and role, \"A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin)\".\n*   **Ticket Resolution Page**\n    *   The ticket resolution page is displayed in the center of the screen.\n    *   The page has a light gray background with black text and a white border.\n    *   The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes in a clear and concise manner.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console with a ticket resolution page open, displaying the ticket number, status, and resolution notes. The page is well-organized and easy to read, with clear headings and concise text. The CoreID Admin Console is displayed in the background, with a dark blue background and white text and icons. Overall, the image provides a clear and detailed view of the ticket resolution page and the CoreID Admin Console.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and